    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7f973990_78f9edf9",
        "filename": "service/src/com/android/server/connectivity/KeepaliveTracker.java",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 631,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-25T06:52:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This contradicts my intuition : usually you\u0027d return true when it works and false when it fails.\n\nBetter : you could return a reason which is probably better both for understandability and for consistency with other methods. This lets you move the logic for whether to cleanup the auto-keepalive to that class, instead of here. The scheme you have now returns something that\u0027s pretty unnatural, really – whether it failed to start \"due to underlying\", but it returns false if it failed for some other reason. This is unnatural and the reason you wrote it like this is that this is what the caller needs to know to figure out whether to clean up. If you return a reason, this code becomes natural, and the code that decides whether to clean up lives in a more logical place.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 631,
        "startChar": 57,
        "endLine": 631,
        "endChar": 72
      },
      "revId": "41f78010a7f6d5dba8dbdbbd6f19182cf26f8672",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d7ee7fa7_e768ab6c",
        "filename": "service/src/com/android/server/connectivity/KeepaliveTracker.java",
        "patchSetId": 10
      },
      "lineNbr": 631,
      "author": {
        "id": 1966927
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-05-30T06:36:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Changed it to return true when it works and false when it fails.\n\nIn this method, unless new reason constants are created, returning a SocketKeepalive reason would not be enough to know that the error that occured stopped the keepalive (the else case intentionally does not stop the keepalive).\nWhether the keepalive is stopped is the information that is important here.\n\n\u003eIf you return a reason, this code becomes natural, and the code that decides whether to clean up lives in a more logical place.\n\nThe problem, I think, is that the code that decides whether to clean up is here already. Or do you suggest removing the handleStopKeepalive from this method (then rely on callers to call the handleStop)?\n\nNow the return value is defined to be \"false if the keepalive is forced to be stopped and was not already STOPPING.\" \nHopefully this is now more natural as it is not forcing the only case to be \"failed to start due to underlying\" and informs of the keepalive being stopped.",
      "parentUuid": "7f973990_78f9edf9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 631,
        "startChar": 57,
        "endLine": 631,
        "endChar": 72
      },
      "revId": "41f78010a7f6d5dba8dbdbbd6f19182cf26f8672",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
